==English==
[[File:Ceres - RC3 - Haulani Crater (22381131691) (cropped).jpg|thumb|The dwarf planet Ceres]]
[[File:Ceres symbol (outline).svg|thumb|Ceres's planetary symbol {{angbr|[[⚳]]}}]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱer-|id=grow}}
{{lbor|en|la|Cerēs}}, goddess of the bounty, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱer-||to grow, to nourish}}. More at [[create]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪəɹiːz/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-Ceres.wav|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|Ce|res}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q32102}}{{lb|en|Roman god}} The Roman [[goddess]] of [[agriculture]]; equivalent to the [[Greek]] goddess [[Demeter]].
# {{senseid|en|Q596}}{{lb|en|astronomy}} A celestial body [[orbit]]ing between [[Mars]] and [[Jupiter]], the  innermost [[dwarf planet]]; officially called '''(1) Ceres'''. (see [[Ceres#Usage notes|usage notes]])
# {{place|en|city|co/Stanislaus County|s/California|c/US}}.
# {{place|en|village|south-east of|x/Cupar|eastern|carea/Fife|cc/Scotland}} {{q|[[OS]] grid ref NO4011}}.

====Usage notes====
Ceres is a dwarf planet, but there is some confusion about whether it is also an asteroid. A NASA webpage states that Vesta, the belt's second-largest object, is the largest asteroid.<ref>{{cite-web |title=Science: One Mission, Two Remarkable Destinations |url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/asteroids-comets-and-meteors/asteroids/overview/?page=0&per_page=40&order=name+asc&search=&condition_1=101%3Aparent_id&condition_2=asteroid%3Abody_type%3Ailike |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200717143754/https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/asteroids-comets-and-meteors/asteroids/overview/?page=0&per_page=40&order=name+asc&search=&condition_1=101:parent_id&condition_2=asteroid:body_type:ilike |archivedate=17 July 2020 |accessdate=14 July 2020 |site=NASA |text=Asteroids range in size from Vesta – the largest at about 329 miles (530&nbsp;km) in diameter{{nbsp}}...}}</ref> The IAU has been equivocal on the subject,<ref name="kenneth">{{Cite book |last=Lang |first=Kenneth |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=S4xDhVCxAQIC&pg=PR5 |title=The Cambridge Guide to the Solar System |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2011 |isbn=978-1-139-49417-5 |pages=372, 442 |accessdate=27 July 2019 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200726125744/https://books.google.com/books?id=S4xDhVCxAQIC&pg=PR5 |archivedate=26 July 2020 }}</ref><ref name="IAU-QA">{{cite-web |title=Question and answers 2 |url=https://www.iau.org/public/themes/pluto/ |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160130022141/http://www.iau.org/public/themes/pluto/ |archivedate=30 January 2016 |accessdate=31 January 2008 |publisher=IAU |text=Ceres is (or now we can say it was) the largest asteroid{{nbsp}}... There are many other asteroids that can come close to the orbital path of Ceres.}}</ref> though its {{w|Minor Planet Center}}, the organisation charged with cataloguing such objects, notes that dwarf planets may have dual designations,<ref>{{cite-web |last=Spahr |first=T. B. |authorlink=Timothy B. Spahr |date=7 September 2006 |title=MPEC 2006-R19: EDITORIAL NOTICE |url=https://minorplanetcenter.net/mpec/K06/K06R19.html |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20081010120050/http://cfa-www.harvard.edu/mpec/K06/K06R19.html |archivedate=10 October 2008 |accessdate=31 January 2008 |publisher=Minor Planet Center |text=the numbering of "dwarf planets" does not preclude their having dual designations in possible separate catalogues of such bodies.}}</ref>
and the joint IAU/[[USGS]]/NASA ''Gazetteer'' categorizes Ceres as both asteroid and a dwarf planet.<ref>{{cite-web |author=IAU |author2=USGS Astrogeology Science Center |author3=NASA |title=Gazetteer of Planetary Nomenclature. Target: Ceres |titleurl=https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Page/CERES/target |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171013231505/https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Page/CERES/target |archivedate=13 October 2017 |accessdate=27 September 2021}}</ref>

